#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199306} :activity/id #uuid "0a27c707-6ea0-5c88-b3ee-852e3b460b3b" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048253 :topic/id #uuid "8c8ae3a5-d4bd-50b8-9f66-a4a042bb870c" :topic/math-academy-id 4143} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199282 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198379 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198021 :question/id #uuid "2a32501f-d0f2-5e59-b7cc-a6b459269f6a" :question/math-academy-id "e-12357"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aaa6b152-86d3-5f96-8a94-57f86a17a84d" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198594} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198615} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278506} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278509} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278522} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278545} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278546} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278547} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278548} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278551} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373972} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401261}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Flux Over a Surface"} :step/id #uuid "034ca782-bf48-515e-9d4a-b38a7f58ef97" :step/math-academy-id 44281} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199295 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198389 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198031 :question/id #uuid "c69a0f41-5b8f-5fcd-b058-94df2d7984bc" :question/math-academy-id "e-12383"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a3486150-b3c2-5faf-99d5-b42ced350996" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048185}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198593} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198610} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278495} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278510} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278518} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373965} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373986} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398514} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398521}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Flux Over a Surface as a Repeated Integral"} :step/id #uuid "3c9cbe38-cd8a-58ac-be23-3f2812d3a11e" :step/math-academy-id 44435 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199300}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199299 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198392 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198034 :question/id #uuid "b81eb0cb-a740-5e86-921c-4baa8c179e55" :question/math-academy-id "e-12387"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "55cd2fdb-4cae-5f0b-bea0-e845d51190e1" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049883}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198602} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198605} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278512} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278515} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278516} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278519} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373979} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401240} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401247} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401254}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Flux Over a Surface as a Repeated Integral Using a Formula"} :step/id #uuid "4ec0f874-f1a4-5c03-8a41-bf094ee7ee97" :step/math-academy-id 44446 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199282}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199300 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199470 :tutorial/content "Let's now derive a general formula for the flux through an *upward-oriented* Cartesian surface.\n\nFor a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v) \\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n=\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}S\n=\n\\iint\\limits_D \\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) \\cdot (\\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v) \\,\\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nConsider a surface $S$ given by the equation $z=g(x,y).$ The canonical parameterization of the surface is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(x,y) = \\left\\langle x,\\: y,\\: g(x,y) \\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nComputing the tangent vectors to the grid curves, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{'} & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,\\frac{∂g}{∂x}⟩ \\\\ {r}_{y}^{'} & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,\\frac{∂g}{∂y}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{'}\\times {r}_{y}^{'} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & \\frac{∂g}{∂x} \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\end{aligned}|=⟨-\\frac{∂g}{∂x},\\,-\\frac{∂g}{∂y},\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the $\\mathbf k$-component is positive, which indicates that our surface is upward-oriented.\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(x,y))\\cdot ({r}_{x}^{'}\\times {r}_{y}^{'})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\frac{∂g}{∂x},\\,-\\frac{∂g}{∂y},\\,1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize as follows:\n\n- *For a surface $S$ given by $z = g(x,y)$ for $(x,y) \\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z) = \\left\\langle P,\\: Q,\\: R \\right\\rangle$ over $S$ is given by*\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n=\n\\iint\\limits_D \\left( -P\\dfrac{\\partial g}{\\partial x} - Q\\dfrac{\\partial g}{\\partial y} + R \\right) \\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe have analogous results for surfaces of the form $y = h(x,z)$ and $x=k(y,z){:}$\n\n- *For a surface $S$ given by $y = h(x,z)$ for $(x,z) \\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z) = \\left\\langle P,\\: Q,\\: R \\right\\rangle$ over $S$ is given by*\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n=\n\\iint\\limits_D \\left( -P\\dfrac{\\partial h}{\\partial x} + Q - R\\dfrac{\\partial h}{\\partial z} \\right) \\textrm{d}A,\n$$\n\n*where the unit normal to the surface is chosen such that the $\\mathbf j$-component is positive.*\n\n- *For a surface $S$ given $x = k(y,z)$ for $(y,z) \\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z) = \\left\\langle P,\\: Q,\\: R \\right\\rangle$ over $S$ is given by*\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n=\n\\iint\\limits_D \\left( P - Q\\dfrac{\\partial k}{\\partial y} - R\\dfrac{\\partial k}{\\partial z} \\right) \\textrm{d}A,\n$$\n\n*where the unit normal to the surface is chosen such that the $\\mathbf i$-component is positive.*" :tutorial/id #uuid "d20203b0-e15f-5061-ae68-16676ec933dc" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7653 :tutorial/title "Formulas for Flux Over Cartesian Surfaces"} :step/id #uuid "299c6f48-c761-519a-8d96-161fea9d6df0" :step/math-academy-id 44449 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199299}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199306 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199473 :tutorial/content "Let's calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=\\left\\langle (x-y)^2, \\:(x+y)^2, \\: 12xy \\right\\rangle\n$$\n\n \n\nthrough the surface $S$ defined by the Cartesian equation \n\n$$\nz=2y-2x\n$$\n\nfor $0 \\leq x \\leq 1$ and $0 \\leq y \\leq 1.$ \n\nFor surfaces like this, substituting $x=u$ and $y=v,$ we obtain a parametrization \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v) = \\left\\langle u,\\: v,\\: 2v-2u \\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nover the domain \n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (u,v) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq u \\leq 1, \\: 0 \\leq v \\leq 1 \\big\\}.\n$$\n\n \n\nRecall that for a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v) \\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n=\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}S\n=\n\\iint\\limits_D \\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) \\cdot (\\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v) \\,\\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2v-2u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,-2⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2v-2u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{'}\\times {r}_{v}^{'} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,-2,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & =⟨(x-y)^{2},\\,(x+y)^{2},\\,12xy⟩{|}_{x=u,\\,\\,y=v,\\,\\,z=2v-2u} \\\\  & =⟨(u-v)^{2},\\,(u+v)^{2},\\,12uv⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $\\mathbf{F}$ and $\\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{'}\\times {r}_{v}^{'}) & =⟨(u-v)^{2},\\,(u+v)^{2},\\,12uv⟩\\cdot ⟨2,\\,-2,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =(u-v)^{2}\\cdot 2+(u+v)^{2}\\cdot (-2)+12uv\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4uv.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $\\mathbf F$ through $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨(x-y)^{2},\\,(x+y)^{2},\\,12xy⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{'}\\times {r}_{v}^{'})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4uv\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}4uv\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2u\\,\\text{d}u\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}2v\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =[{u}^{2}]_{0}^{1}\\cdot [{v}^{2}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =({1}^{2}-{0}^{2})\\cdot ({1}^{2}-{0}^{2}) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "f32d6993-cd62-5fe7-98f3-9a89a815dbfc" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7666 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "dd32837c-4771-57ff-abbc-360334e17eeb" :step/math-academy-id 44522 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199295}}] :activity/title "Calculating Flux Through Cartesian Surfaces" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186198485}]}
